// A volatile write may not be lifted out of the branch that guards it.
//
// relaxed-write-lifted.lit is the same program without the volatile
// annotations on the writes: there both arms write 1 to y, lifting merges the
// two writes into one justified whatever the branch decides, and the write
// loses its dependency on the read of x. A volatile access stays where the
// program put it, so here the write stays under the branch and the dependency
// stands in every execution.
//
// Volatile also keeps an access from being elided; see
// volatile-store-not-elided.lit.
//
// The read of x is volatile so that it cannot be forwarded from x := 0. A
// forwarded read drops its dependency too, and the assertion counts a
// forwarded read as executed, so forwarding would remove (3,5) from .dp
// without any lifting and the assertion could not tell the two apart. A
// volatile read before the branch does not stop lifting: the control
// relaxed-write-lifted.lit shows the dependency removed with the same read.
x := 0;
y := 0;
r1 :v= x;
if (r1 = 0) {
  y :v= 1;
} else {
  y :v= 1;
}
%% forbid ((3,5) ∉ .dp) []
